        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 1 &middot; TWO RIVERS, ONE BORDER</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">One Country Becomes One State</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">For nine years, Texas was its own country, the Republic of Texas. In 1845 it joined the United States. This is called <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="When one country takes in land or another country and makes it part of itself.">annexation</a>. Texas became the twenty-eighth state.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Yet one question came along with it. Where did Texas end in the southwest? Texas said its <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A line that shows where one place or country ends and another begins.">boundary</a> was the <strong>Big River</strong> (<em>R&iacute;o Grande</em>). In 1836 the Republic&rsquo;s congress had passed a law naming that river as its border. Mexico gave a different answer. Mexico had never accepted that Texas was a separate country. Mexico said the old border of its Texas province was the <strong>Nut River</strong> (<em>R&iacute;o Nueces</em>), farther to the north.</p><div id="foreign" style="background: #E8F6FB; border-left: 5px solid #2196D0; border-top: 3px solid #55C8E8; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 20px; margin: 14px 0;"><div style="font-size: 11px; color: #1B6A94; margin-bottom: 8px;">&#128161; FOREIGN LANGUAGE VOCABULARY</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Matamoros</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Matamoros" onclick="speakGloss(event,'mat uh mor ohs')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(mat-uh-MOR-ohs)</span>: A Mexican city on the south bank of the Río Grande.</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Nueces</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Nueces" onclick="speakGloss(event,'nway ses')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(NWAY-ses)</span>: Spanish for &quot;nuts.&quot; The name of the Nut River.</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Río Grande</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Río Grande" onclick="speakGloss(event,'ree oh gran day')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(REE-oh GRAN-day)</span>: Spanish for &quot;Big River.&quot;</div></div><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Between the two rivers lay a wide strip of dry brush country. People call it the <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="NWAY-ses. Spanish for &quot;nuts.&quot; The name of the Nut River."><em>Nueces</em></a> Strip. Both countries said it was theirs, so it was <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Argued over. Each side says it is right.">disputed</a> land.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
