</div><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Who Really Held the Land?</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Not Texas. Texans had never really governed the strip. A few Americans lived near Corpus Christi, on the coast. But Mexican ranchers and towns stood along the <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="REE-oh GRAN-day. Spanish for &quot;Big River.&quot;"><em>Río Grande</em></a>, such as the city of <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="mat-uh-MOR-ohs. A Mexican city on the south bank of the Río Grande."><em>Matamoros</em></a>.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">When the United States Congress voted to annex Texas, it did not settle the question. It left the exact border to be worked out with Mexico later. Mexico cut off its ties with the United States over the annexation. The border had become a problem for the whole country.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">That changed Texas&rsquo;s position. As a republic, Texas had faced Mexico alone. As a state, it could count on the United States Army. But it gave up something too. The choice between talking and fighting now belonged to the president and Congress in Washington, not to Texans.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 2 &middot; POLK SENDS THE ARMY</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">A President Who Wanted More</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The new president was James K. Polk of Tennessee. He took office in March 1845. Polk was an <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A person who wants their country to grow larger by gaining more land.">expansionist</a>. He wanted the United States to grow, and he wanted it to happen while he was in office. Many Americans felt the same way. That year a writer named John O&rsquo;Sullivan gave the feeling a name, <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="The belief, common in the 1800s, that the United States was meant to spread across North America.">Manifest Destiny</a>.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Polk wanted more than the R&iacute;o Grande. He also wanted California. He sent a diplomat, John Slidell, to Mexico City to offer money for California and to settle the border. Mexico&rsquo;s leaders refused to receive him.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Mexican leaders had their own reasons. To many Mexicans, Texas was a province that had rebelled, and its loss still hurt national pride. Accepting the R&iacute;o Grande as a border would mean giving up even more land. Any Mexican president who seemed ready to deal with Slidell could be attacked as a traitor. In December 1845, President Herrera was overthrown, partly because people thought he was willing to talk. His successors were less willing.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Army Moves South</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Polk also sent soldiers. In the summer of 1845, General Zachary Taylor led an army to Texas. His camp was at Corpus Christi, on the northern edge of the disputed land. The army&rsquo;s job was to protect Texas.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In March 1846, Polk gave a new order. Taylor was to march all the way to the <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="REE-oh GRAN-day. Spanish for &quot;Big River.&quot;"><em>Río Grande</em></a>. He built a fort across the river from <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="mat-uh-MOR-ohs. A Mexican city on the south bank of the Río Grande."><em>Matamoros</em></a>. To Americans, this was defending Texas. To Mexico, it was an army marching into Mexico. Mexican officers told Taylor to leave. He stayed.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">On April 25, 1846, Mexican soldiers attacked an American patrol north of the river. Several Americans were killed and the rest were captured. On May 11, Polk sent a message to Congress. He said Mexico &ldquo;has invaded our territory and shed American blood upon the American soil.&rdquo; Two days later, Congress declared war.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">But whose soil was it? That was the whole question. Some Americans doubted Polk&rsquo;s claim. A new congressman from Illinois, Abraham Lincoln, later asked Polk to name the exact spot where the blood had been shed.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">What Might Have Been</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Historians like to ask what would have happened if Taylor had stayed at the Nueces River in 1846. The clash of April 25 happened because two armies were standing face to face along the R&iacute;o Grande. If Taylor had stayed farther north, that clash would not have happened in the same place at the same time. But Mexico still refused to accept Texas, and Polk still wanted California. War might have come anyway, later or from a different spark. Historians cannot be sure, but the question shows how much one order could matter.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; WHO CHOSE WHAT?</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Each row names a choice from the road to war. Pick the person or group who made it.</div>
